¶
'''unmitigable''' (un-mit′i-ga-bl), ''a.'' Not capable of being mitigated, softened, or lessened.

{{smaller block|Her most ''unmitigable'' rage. {{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, i. 2. 276.}}}}

'''unmitigated''' (un-mit′i-gā-ted), ''a.'' Not mitigated; not lessened; not softened or toned down; unassuaged; often, especially in colloquial use, unconscionable: as, an ''unmitigated'' scoundrel; an ''unmitigated'' lie.

{{smaller block|With public accusation, uncovered slander, ''unmitigated'' rancour. {{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, iv. 1. 308.}}
The ''unmitigated'' blaze of vindicatory law. {{right|''J. W. Alexander'', Discourses, p. 62.}}}}

'''unmitigatedly''' (un-mit′i-gā-ted-li), ''adv.'' Without mitigation; in an unmitigated degree; utterly.

{{smaller block|“Lady Delmar” is neither realistic nor idealistic; it is altogether improbable and ''unmitigatedly'' melodramatic. {{right|''The Academy'', April 11, 1891, p. 342.}}}}

'''unmixed, unmixt''' (un-mikst′), ''a.'' Not mixed; not mingled; pure; simple; unadulterated; unmingled; unalloyed.

{{smaller block|<poem>Thy commandment all alone shall live, . . .
''Unmix’d'' with baser matter.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 5. 104.}}
God is an ''unmixed'' good. {{right|''T. Brooks'', Works, I. 187.}}}}

'''unmixedly''' (un-mik′sed-li), ''adv.'' Entirely; purely; without mixture of other qualities; utterly. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|That superstition cannot be regarded as ''unmixedly'' noxious which compels the hereditary master to kneel before the spiritual tribunal of the hereditary bondman. {{right|''Macaulay.''}}}}

'''unmoaned''' (un-mōnd′), ''a.'' Not bemoaned or lamented.

readings.c1s2:
lamented.

{{smaller block|Our fatherless distress was left ''unmoan’d.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., ii. 2. 64.}}}}

'''unmodernize''' (un-mod′ėr-nīz), ''v. t.'' To alter from a modern fashion or style; give an ancient or old-fashioned form or fashion to.

{{smaller block|''Unmodernize'' a poem rather than give it an antique air.
{{right|''Lamb'', Essays.}}}}

'''unmodifiable''' (un-mod′i-fī-a̤-bl), ''a.'' Not modifiable; not capable of being modified.

'''unmodifiableness''' (un-mod′i-fī-a̤-bl-nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being unmodifiable.

{{smaller block|A nature not of brutish ''unmodifiableness.''
{{right|''George Eliot'', Daniel Deronda, lviii. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''unmodified''' (un-mod′i-fīd), ''a.'' Not modified; not altered in form; not qualified in meaning; not limited or circumscribed.

{{smaller block|An universal, ''unmodified'' capacity to which the fanatics pretend. {{right|''Burke'', To Sir H. Langrishe.}}}}

'''unmodish''' (un-mō′dish), ''a.'' Not modish; not according to custom or fashion; unfashionable; not stylish.

{{smaller block|Your Eloquence would be needless—’tis so ''unmodish'' to need Persuasion. {{right|''Steele'', Tender Husband, v. 1.}}
<poem>Who there frequents at these ''unmodish'' hours,
But ancient matrons with their frizzled towers,
And gray religious maids?</poem>
{{right|''Gay'', Eclogues, The Toilette.}}}}

'''unmoistened''' (un-moi′snd), ''a.'' Not made moist or humid; not wetted; dry.

{{smaller block|<poem>And mayst thou die with an ''unmoisten’d'' eye,
And no tear follow thee!</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher (and another?)'', Nice Valour, ii. 1.}}}}

'''unmold, unmould''' (un-mōld′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> +

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|This is an important point, as suggesting the disinterested and ''unmonopolising'' side of æsthetic pleasure.
{{right|''J. Sully'', Encyc. Brit., I. 216.}}}}

'''unmoor''' (un-mör′), ''v.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''moor''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' ''Naut.'', to bring to the state of riding with a single anchor, after having been moored by two or more cables.—'''2.''' To loose from anchorage or from moorings, literally or figuratively.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thy skiff ''unmoor'',
And waft us from the silent shore.</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Giaour.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To loose from moorings; weigh anchor.
{{smaller block|<poem>Look, where beneath the castle grey
His fleet ''unmoor'' from Aros bay!</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Lord of the Isles, i. 12.}}}}

'''unmoral''' (un-mor′al), ''a.'' Not moral; non-moral; not a subject of moral attributes; neither moral nor immoral.

'''unmorality''' (un-mọ̄-ral′ị-ti), ''n.'' Absence of morality; unmoral character.
{{smaller block|The picture is very highly, a trifle too highly, wrought: but what pathos for those who can see behind it! The need of counsel, the lack of previous education, the absolute ''unmorality''. {{right|''The Academy'', Feb. 8, 1890, p. 94.}}}}

'''unmoralized''' (un-mor′al-īzd), ''a.'' '''1.''' Untutored by morality; not conformed to good morals. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|A dissolute and ''unmoralized'' temper. {{right|''Norris.''}}}}
'''2.''' Not subjected to moralizing consideration: as, an ''unmoralized'' thought.
{{smaller block|There are no cabinets of ''unmoralised'' or half-moralised conceptions, serving as illustrations of the evolution hypothesis. {{right|''New Princeton Rev.'', I. 180.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
Also ''unmoralised.''

'''unmoralizing''' (un-mor′al-ī-zing), ''a.'' '''1.''' Demoralizing.—'''2.''' Not given to or consisting in moral reflections.

{{smaller block|He was primarily the artist, impersonal, ''unmoralizing,'' an eye and a vocabulary. {{right|''The Atlantic,'' LXIV. 701.}}}}

'''unmorrised''' (un-mor′ist), ''a.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''morris'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Not dressed as a morris-dancer; not disguised by such a dress. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|<poem>What ails this fellow,
Thus to appear before me ''unmorrised?''</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher,'' Women Pleased, iv. 1.}}}}

'''unmortise''' (un-môr′tis), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''mortise.''] To loosen or undo as a mortise; loosen the mortises or joints of.

{{smaller block|In a dark nook stood an old broken-bottomed cane-couch, without a squab or cover-lid, sunk at one corner, and ''unmortised'' by the failing of one of its worm-eaten legs. {{right|''Richardson,'' Clarissa Harlowe, VI. 304.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>The wrist is parted from the hand that waved,
The feet ''unmortised'' from their ankle-bones.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson,'' Merlin and Vivien.}}}}

'''un-Mosaic''' (un-mọ̄-zā′ik), ''a.'' The reverse of Mosaic; contrary to Moses or his law.

{{smaller block|By this reckoning Moses should be most ''un-Mosaic.'' {{right|''Milton.''}}}}

'''unmothered''' (un-muᴛ̵ʜ′ėrd), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not having a mother; deprived of a mother. [Rare.]—'''2†.''' Not having the feelings of a mother.

{{smaller block|<poem>I e’en quake to proceede. My spirit turnes edge.
I feare me she’s ''unmother’d,'' yet I’ll venture.</poem>
{{right|''C. Tourneur,'' Revenger’s Tragedy, ii. 1.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''unmotherly''' (un-muᴛ̵ʜ′ėr-li), ''a.'' Not resembling or not befitting a mother.
{{smaller block|''Unmotherly'' mother and unwomanly<br>Woman, that near turns motherhood to shame,<br>Womanliness to loathing.
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, II. 195.}}}}

'''unmould''', ''v. t.'' See ''unmold.''

'''unmounted''' (un-moun′ted), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not mounted; not performing regular duties on horseback: as, ''unmounted'' police.—'''2.''' Not furnished or set with appropriate or necessary appurtenances: as, an ''unmounted'' jewel; not affixed to a mount or backing, as of stiff paper or cardboard, as a drawing or a photograph; not provided with a mat of appropriate size and covered with a protecting glass, as a lantern-slide or transparency.

'''unmourned''' (un-mōrnd′), ''a.'' Not mourned; not grieved for or lamented.
{{smaller block|But still he goes ''unmourn’d'', returns unsought,<br>And oft, when present, absent from my thought.
{{right|''Byron'', Corsair, ii. 14.}}}}

'''unmovability†''' (un-mö-va-bil′i-ti), ''n.'' [ME. ''unmoevablete''; as ''unmovable'' + ''-ity''.] Immovability. Also ''unmoveability.''
{{smaller block|It is constreyned into symplicite, that is to seyn, into ''unmoevablete.'' {{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, iv. prose 6.}}}}

'''unmovable''' (un-mö′va-bl), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unmoevable'', ''unmevable''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''movable.''] Immovable. Also ''unmoveable.''
{{smaller block|It is clept the dede See, for it rennethe nought, but is evere ''unmevable.'' {{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 100.}}}}
